// Design-layer spec for CaseGraphen issue #34 — what "this requirement is
// satisfied" is allowed to mean.
//
// What this formalizes: `NativeEvaluationContext::compute_satisfied_requirement_ids`
// in `src/native_eval.rs` and the `requirement_satisfied` flag it stamps on a
// `NativeReviewGap` (`src/native_eval/sections.rs`). That flag is read by the
// Assurance axis, the close check, `native_halt`'s `needs_review`, and the text
// renderer — four consumers of one boolean.
//
// The defect: a hard `requires_evidence` edge belongs to a *holder*. Whether it
// is discharged is decided per holder — `evidence_requirement_satisfied(holder,
// requirement)`, whose third disjunct tests coverage of the *holder's* id. The
// shipped derivation asks that question of every holder and then keeps the
// union, so one holder's satisfaction marked the requirement satisfied for all
// of them. Reproduced end to end in issue #34: `evidence:x`'s gap reported
// `requirement_satisfied: true` by way of holder `evidence:a`, while `work:w2`,
// which also hard-requires `evidence:x`, was correctly still blocked.
//
// Two candidate meanings are modelled side by side and compared, because the
// interesting content of this decision is in the comparison:
//
//   satisfied_for_some()  the shipped union      — "discharged for someone"
//   satisfied_for_all()   the decision taken     — "discharged for every holder"
//
// Both stay live in this file on purpose. `SingleHolderAgreement` proves they
// are the same answer whenever a requirement has exactly one holder, which is
// the compatibility claim the change has to make and the reason no existing
// single-holder fixture moves.
//
// REVISION HISTORY, because the correction is the most useful thing here.
//
//   1. The first version had no `is_subject` — every `Holder` was implicitly a
//      cell readiness evaluates. That is not the system: `readiness_subject`
//      excludes evidence/review/projection/revision/morphism/external-ref
//      cells and every lifecycle in Resolved/Accepted/Retired/Rejected/
//      Superseded, so those cells never produce a `missing_evidence`
//      obstruction however unsatisfied their edge is. Meanwhile the shipped
//      `compute_satisfied_requirement_ids` ranged over *all* cells.
//
//      Under the union that asymmetry was harmless in the safe direction — an
//      extra holder could only add satisfaction. The every-holder rule this
//      file specifies inverted it: an extra holder could only subtract it. So
//      `cell transition --to resolved` on one holder, the ordinary way work
//      finishes, made the case space refuse to close with **no obstruction
//      anywhere naming why** — `space reason` clean, `close check` refusing,
//      `assurance` reading `review_required`, and `needs_review` pointing at a
//      cell nothing explains. Found by an invariant-duplication audit,
//      reproduced, and fixed by giving `compute_satisfied_requirement_ids` the
//      same `readiness_subject` filter obstruction production already uses.
//
//      This spec returned `proved` throughout. It proved a true property of a
//      model in which every holder is evaluated, and shipped a machine in which
//      they are not — the same failure `operate-halt.fsl`'s revision history
//      records three times. A proof over a model simpler than the system gives
//      assurance it has not earned, which is worse than silence, and the reason
//      it keeps happening is that the omission is invisible from inside the
//      model: nothing in `INV-EVID-001` hints that `Holder` is missing a case.
//
// Modelling bound (representation only, no behavioural content): one
// requirement and three candidate holders, the smallest shape in which a
// requirement can be discharged for one holder and not another.

spec CaseGraphenRequirementSatisfaction {
  // Cells that might hold a hard `requires_evidence` edge into the one
  // requirement this model is about.
  type Holder = 0..2

  state {
    // Does this cell hold a hard `requires_evidence` edge into the requirement?
    // Authored structure, so it can appear and disappear as the graph evolves.
    holds_edge: Map<Holder, Bool>,
    // A review promoted evidence naming the requirement itself. Holder-
    // independent: this is `trusted_evidence_ids` / `trusted_coverage_targets`
    // matching the requirement id.
    trusted_for_requirement: Bool,
    // A review promoted evidence recorded as covering this *holder*. This is
    // `trusted_coverage_targets.contains(cell_id)` — the disjunct that makes
    // satisfaction holder-dependent, and therefore the whole reason a union
    // over holders loses information.
    trusted_for_holder: Map<Holder, Bool>,
    // Does readiness evaluate this cell at all? `readiness_subject`
    // (`src/native_eval.rs`) excludes evidence/review/projection/revision/
    // morphism/external-ref cells, and every cell whose lifecycle is
    // Resolved/Accepted/Retired/Rejected/Superseded. A cell it excludes never
    // produces a `missing_evidence` obstruction, however unsatisfied its edge
    // is.
    //
    // The first version of this spec had no such notion — every `Holder` was
    // implicitly evaluated — and that omission is not academic: it is why
    // `INV-EVID-001` was proved of a machine simpler than the one built, and
    // the shipped `compute_satisfied_requirement_ids` ranged over *all* cells
    // while obstruction production ranged only over subjects. Under the old
    // union that asymmetry could only add satisfaction; after the every-holder
    // rule it could only subtract it, so `cell transition --to resolved` on
    // one holder made the case space refuse to close with no obstruction
    // anywhere naming why. Found by an invariant-duplication audit and
    // reproduced.
    is_subject: Map<Holder, Bool>
  }

  init {
    forall h: Holder {
      holds_edge[h] = false
      trusted_for_holder[h] = false
      is_subject[h] = true
    }
    trusted_for_requirement = false
  }

  // `evidence_requirement_satisfied(holder, requirement)`, per holder. This is
  // the one place the question is answered, and both candidate aggregates below
  // are defined in terms of it rather than restating it.
  def satisfied_for(h: Holder) = trusted_for_requirement or trusted_for_holder[h]

  // A holder whose hard edge is not discharged. This is what readiness reports
  // as a `missing_evidence` obstruction, and it is holder-scoped in the shipped
  // code — the readiness layer was never wrong.
  def blocked(h: Holder) = is_subject[h] and holds_edge[h] and not satisfied_for(h)

  // The holders whose unsatisfied edge the evaluation would actually report.
  // Both aggregates below range over exactly these, so "which holders count"
  // is decided once — the duplication that produced the defect was two
  // answers to this question, not two answers to `satisfied_for`.
  def counts(h: Holder) = is_subject[h] and holds_edge[h]

  // THE SHIPPED UNION. Kept live, not deleted: `FlatUnionIsNotHolderSound`
  // below is the formal statement of issue #34's reproduction, and it needs
  // this definition to say anything.
  def satisfied_for_some() = exists h: Holder { counts(h) and satisfied_for(h) }

  // THE DECISION (issue #34, chosen 2026-08-02). Note the left conjunct: an
  // unguarded `forall` over holders is vacuously true for a requirement nothing
  // requires, which would turn "no one requires this" into "everyone's
  // requirement is discharged" — the union answered that case with `false`, so
  // dropping the guard would be a regression introduced by the fix.
  def satisfied_for_all() =
    (exists h: Holder { counts(h) })
    and (forall h: Holder { counts(h) => satisfied_for(h) })

  // ------------------------------------------------------- how state moves

  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-001", "a hard requires_evidence edge may be authored")
  fair action author_requirement_edge(h: Holder) {
    requires not holds_edge[h]
    holds_edge[h] = true
  }

  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-002", "a hard requires_evidence edge may be retired")
  fair action retire_requirement_edge(h: Holder) {
    requires holds_edge[h]
    holds_edge[h] = false
  }

  // `review accept` on evidence that names the requirement itself.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-003", "a review may promote evidence covering the requirement")
  fair action promote_for_requirement() {
    requires not trusted_for_requirement
    trusted_for_requirement = true
  }

  // `review accept` on evidence whose recorded coverage names one holder. This
  // is the act that can discharge the edge for one cell and not another.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-004", "a review may promote evidence covering one holder only")
  fair action promote_for_holder(h: Holder) {
    requires not trusted_for_holder[h]
    trusted_for_holder[h] = true
  }

  // The ordinary way work finishes. It is a *lifecycle* act with no relation
  // to evidence, which is exactly what made the defect surprising: nothing
  // about resolving `work:w2` says anything about `evidence:x`, yet before the
  // fix it changed that requirement's reported satisfaction.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-009", "a holder can leave the set readiness evaluates")
  fair action resolve_holder(h: Holder) {
    requires is_subject[h]
    is_subject[h] = false
  }

  // ------------------------------------------------------------ properties

  // The property the decision buys, and the one every consumer of the flag
  // assumes without checking: if the evaluation says the requirement is
  // satisfied, no cell is still blocked on it. A consumer that joins this flag
  // to a holder-scoped finding — which is what the #24 renderer did, producing
  // one line asserting both halves of a contradiction — is then making a sound
  // join rather than an unsound one.
  @requirement("INV-EVID-001", "a satisfied requirement leaves no holder blocked on it")
  invariant HolderSoundness {
    satisfied_for_all() => (not exists h: Holder { blocked(h) })
  }

  // Issue #34's reproduction, as a reachable state rather than as a failing
  // invariant, because the union is no longer what the code computes and a spec
  // that fails is not a spec anyone keeps running. Read it as: the shipped
  // meaning could say "satisfied" while a holder was demonstrably blocked.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-005", "the union could claim satisfaction while a holder was blocked")
  reachable FlatUnionIsNotHolderSound {
    satisfied_for_some() and (exists h: Holder { blocked(h) })
  }

  // The guard on `satisfied_for_all`'s left conjunct, stated so it cannot be
  // dropped by a later simplification: nothing is discharged for a requirement
  // no cell requires.
  @requirement("INV-EVID-002", "a requirement nobody holds is not satisfied")
  invariant NoVacuousSatisfaction {
    satisfied_for_all() => (exists h: Holder { holds_edge[h] })
  }

  // The compatibility claim, proved rather than asserted: where exactly one
  // cell holds the edge, the two meanings are the same answer. This is why the
  // change moves no existing single-holder fixture, and it is the thing to
  // re-check if either definition is ever edited.
  @requirement("INV-EVID-003", "with one holder, the union and the decision agree")
  invariant SingleHolderAgreement {
    (exists h: Holder {
      holds_edge[h] and (forall g: Holder { holds_edge[g] => g == h })
    })
    => (satisfied_for_some() == satisfied_for_all())
  }

  // The property the audit's reproduction violated, and the one that makes the
  // flag safe for the close check and the Assurance axis to consume: if the
  // requirement reads unsatisfied while some evaluated holder requires it,
  // then some evaluated holder is blocked — so an obstruction exists naming
  // why. Without the `counts` guard on `satisfied_for_all` this is false, and
  // the witness is a resolved or retired holder: zero obstructions anywhere,
  // `close:native-review-gaps-closed` failing, and `assurance` reading
  // `review_required` with nothing to act on.
  @requirement("INV-EVID-004", "an unsatisfied requirement always has a blocked holder to explain it")
  invariant NoUnexplainedUnsatisfied {
    ((exists h: Holder { counts(h) }) and not satisfied_for_all())
      => (exists h: Holder { blocked(h) })
  }

  // The shape that produced the defect, kept reachable so the fix is not
  // silently reverted: a holder outside readiness has an unsatisfied edge, and
  // the requirement still reads satisfied because that holder cannot block.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-010", "a non-evaluated holder's unsatisfied edge does not veto")
  reachable NonSubjectHolderDoesNotVeto {
    exists h: Holder {
      exists g: Holder {
        h != g and counts(h) and satisfied_for(h)
          and holds_edge[g] and not is_subject[g] and not satisfied_for(g)
          and satisfied_for_all()
      }
    }
  }

  // Both answers must stay reachable, or the flag is a constant.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-006", "a requirement can be satisfied for every holder")
  reachable SatisfiedIsReachable { satisfied_for_all() }

  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-007", "a requirement can be unsatisfied while held")
  reachable UnsatisfiedIsReachable {
    (exists h: Holder { holds_edge[h] }) and not satisfied_for_all()
  }

  // The shape that made the two meanings diverge in the real reproduction: one
  // holder discharged through its own coverage, another still blocked.
  @requirement("MODEL-EVID-008", "one holder can be discharged while another is not")
  reachable HoldersCanDisagree {
    exists h: Holder {
      exists g: Holder {
        h != g and holds_edge[h] and holds_edge[g] and satisfied_for(h) and blocked(g)
      }
    }
  }

  // Every state of this model is a legitimate resting point: it describes what
  // an evaluation says about a graph, not a process that must make progress.
  terminal { true }
}

verify {
}

// ------------------------------------------------------- CORRESPONDENCE
//
// This file proves a design-layer property by k-induction. `fslc verify`
// returning `proved` is a claim about the model above; it is NOT evidence
// about the shipped binary — this file's own REVISION HISTORY records that
// exact failure once already (issue #37): the first version proved
// `INV-EVID-001` of a machine in which every `Holder` was implicitly a
// readiness subject, while the shipped code excluded most of them, and the
// case space could refuse to close with no obstruction naming why. This
// section names the real tests that carry each requirement's claim into the
// shipped code, mechanically checked by `tests/spec_correspondence.rs`,
// which parses the `@carries-test` lines below and asserts every named test
// still exists. It proves nothing about whether those tests pass, only that
// the claim "this test carries this requirement" is not stale.
//
// Marker syntax: a line `// @carries-test <TAG> <path>::<fn>` names a real
// test exercising the tagged requirement; a tag may have more than one such
// line. A line `// @carries-test <TAG> NONE` states explicitly that nothing
// in the test tree carries that requirement today.
//
// MODEL-EVID-001 (a hard requires_evidence edge may be authored): every
// holder-based test authors such an edge as fixture setup, but the cited
// test is the one that also asserts the read result changes because the
// edge exists — authoring is not merely scaffolding there, it is part of
// what is checked.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-001 src/native_eval/tests.rs::a_single_holder_requirement_reads_identically_satisfied_or_not
//
// MODEL-EVID-002 (a hard requires_evidence edge may be retired): NONE. Every
// test that removes a holder from consideration does so by changing the
// *holder cell's* lifecycle (`resolve_holder`, MODEL-EVID-009 below); no
// test retires the `requires_evidence` *relation* itself and observes the
// requirement's read change as a result.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-002 NONE
//
// MODEL-EVID-003 (a review may promote evidence covering the requirement
// itself) and MODEL-EVID-004 (a review may promote evidence covering one
// holder only): the same property test's generator drives both — case `1`
// records coverage against the requirement id directly
// (`trusted_for_requirement`), case `2` against a specific holder id
// (`trusted_for_holder`) — over arbitrary holder counts and shapes. The
// CLI-level mechanism both compile down to (`review accept` promoting
// `--satisfies` targets) is exercised end to end, without decomposing which
// disjunct fired, by the second test cited.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-003 src/native_eval/tests.rs::satisfied_requirement_ids_never_names_a_requirement_with_a_blocking_missing_evidence_obstruction
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-004 src/native_eval/tests.rs::satisfied_requirement_ids_never_names_a_requirement_with_a_blocking_missing_evidence_obstruction
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-004 tests/command.rs::review_accept_reports_the_coverage_it_activates
//
// MODEL-EVID-009 (a holder can leave the set readiness evaluates): this is
// `resolve_holder`, and the cited test is issue #34's exact regression case —
// parametrized over the second holder staying `Active`, going `Retired`, or
// going `Resolved`, and asserting how the read and the close check change.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-009 src/native_eval/tests.rs::second_holder_leaving_the_readiness_subject_set_changes_whether_the_shared_requirement_reads_satisfied
//
// INV-EVID-001 (HolderSoundness — a satisfied requirement leaves no holder
// blocked on it):
// @carries-test INV-EVID-001 src/native_eval/tests.rs::satisfied_requirement_ids_never_names_a_requirement_with_a_blocking_missing_evidence_obstruction
//
// MODEL-EVID-005 (FlatUnionIsNotHolderSound — the union could claim
// satisfaction while a holder was blocked): kept live in the model as the
// formal statement of issue #34's reproduction, not as something the shipped
// code still does. The cited test is the regression proof that the CURRENT
// code does NOT do this — issue #34's exact fixture, asserting the
// once-wrongly-satisfied gap now reads `!requirement_satisfied`.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-005 src/native_eval/tests.rs::compute_satisfied_requirement_ids_requires_every_holder
//
// INV-EVID-002 (NoVacuousSatisfaction — a requirement nobody holds is not
// satisfied):
// @carries-test INV-EVID-002 src/native_eval/tests.rs::a_requirement_nobody_holds_is_not_satisfied
//
// INV-EVID-003 (SingleHolderAgreement — with one holder, the union and the
// decision agree):
// @carries-test INV-EVID-003 src/native_eval/tests.rs::a_single_holder_requirement_reads_identically_satisfied_or_not
//
// INV-EVID-004 (NoUnexplainedUnsatisfied — an unsatisfied requirement always
// has a blocked holder to explain it): the same property test cited under
// INV-EVID-001 asserts both invariants in the same run; the deterministic
// regression test additionally isolates the exact defect shape (a resolved
// or retired second holder leaving zero obstructions anywhere).
// @carries-test INV-EVID-004 src/native_eval/tests.rs::satisfied_requirement_ids_never_names_a_requirement_with_a_blocking_missing_evidence_obstruction
// @carries-test INV-EVID-004 src/native_eval/tests.rs::second_holder_leaving_the_readiness_subject_set_changes_whether_the_shared_requirement_reads_satisfied
//
// MODEL-EVID-010 (NonSubjectHolderDoesNotVeto — a non-evaluated holder's
// unsatisfied edge does not veto): the same regression test's `Retired`/
// `Resolved` branches are exactly this shape — a holder outside readiness
// whose edge is unsatisfied, alongside a satisfied evaluated holder, with
// the requirement still reading satisfied overall.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-010 src/native_eval/tests.rs::second_holder_leaving_the_readiness_subject_set_changes_whether_the_shared_requirement_reads_satisfied
//
// MODEL-EVID-006 (SatisfiedIsReachable) and MODEL-EVID-007
// (UnsatisfiedIsReachable): the cited test's two halves (`satisfied`/
// `unsatisfied` fixtures) witness both reachability claims directly.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-006 src/native_eval/tests.rs::a_single_holder_requirement_reads_identically_satisfied_or_not
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-007 src/native_eval/tests.rs::a_single_holder_requirement_reads_identically_satisfied_or_not
//
// MODEL-EVID-008 (HoldersCanDisagree — one holder discharged while another is
// not): issue #34's exact reproduction has this shape — holder `evidence:a`
// is discharged via its own coverage while holder `work:w2` is genuinely
// blocked on the same requirement.
// @carries-test MODEL-EVID-008 src/native_eval/tests.rs::compute_satisfied_requirement_ids_requires_every_holder
